WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:05.296 --> 00:09.146 align:middle
Transcript of Chair Powell's
Press Conference June 15,

00:09.286 --> 00:11.146 align:middle
2022 CHAIR POWELL.

00:11.146 --> 00:11.766 align:middle
Good afternoon.

00:11.846 --> 00:14.246 align:middle
I will begin with one
overarching message:

00:15.406 --> 00:17.406 align:middle
We at the Fed understand
the hardship

00:17.466 --> 00:18.876 align:middle
that high inflation is causing.

00:19.426 --> 00:22.626 align:middle
We are strongly committed to
bringing inflation back down,

00:22.826 --> 00:25.296 align:middle
and we're moving
expeditiously to do so.

00:26.766 --> 00:28.646 align:middle
We have both the tools
we need and the resolve

00:28.646 --> 00:31.216 align:middle
that it will take to restore
price stability on behalf

00:31.216 --> 00:33.176 align:middle
of American families
and businesses.

00:35.046 --> 00:37.086 align:middle
The economy and the country
have been through a lot

00:37.176 --> 00:39.746 align:middle
over the past two and a half
years and have proved resilient.

00:40.146 --> 00:43.296 align:middle
It is essential that we bring
inflation down if we are

00:43.296 --> 00:44.626 align:middle
to have a sustained period

00:44.756 --> 00:47.336 align:middle
of strong labor market
conditions that benefit all.

00:48.976 --> 00:51.596 align:middle
From the standpoint of
our congressional mandate

00:51.596 --> 00:53.816 align:middle
to promote maximum
employment and price stability,

00:54.696 --> 00:56.196 align:middle
the current picture
is plain to see:

00:57.316 --> 00:58.996 align:middle
The labor market
is extremely tight,

00:59.576 --> 01:00.916 align:middle
and inflation is much too high.

01:02.526 --> 01:05.566 align:middle
Against this backdrop, today the
Federal Open Market Committee

01:05.566 --> 01:09.066 align:middle
raised its policy interest
rate by   percentage point

01:10.136 --> 01:12.416 align:middle
and anticipates that
ongoing increases

01:12.416 --> 01:13.696 align:middle
in that rate will
be appropriate.

01:14.766 --> 01:17.266 align:middle
In addition, we are
continuing the process

01:17.266 --> 01:19.836 align:middle
of significantly reducing the
size of our balance sheet.

01:21.106 --> 01:23.706 align:middle
I'll have more to say about
today's monetary policy actions

01:23.706 --> 01:26.126 align:middle
after briefly reviewing
economic developments.

01:28.426 --> 01:31.736 align:middle
Overall economic activity edged
down in the first quarter,

01:31.736 --> 01:35.406 align:middle
as unusually sharp swings in
inventories and net exports more

01:35.406 --> 01:37.936 align:middle
than offset continued
strong underlying demand.

01:39.076 --> 01:41.826 align:middle
Recent indicators suggest that
real GDP growth has picked

01:41.826 --> 01:42.526 align:middle
up this quarter,

01:43.246 --> 01:45.276 align:middle
with consumption
spending remaining strong.

01:46.666 --> 01:51.626 align:middle
In contrast, growth in business
fixed investment appears

01:51.626 --> 01:54.676 align:middle
to be slowing, and activity
in the housing sector looks

01:54.676 --> 01:56.976 align:middle
to be softening, in
part reflecting higher

01:56.976 --> 01:57.686 align:middle
mortgage rates.

01:59.036 --> 02:01.466 align:middle
The tightening in financial
conditions that we've seen

02:01.466 --> 02:04.046 align:middle
in recent months should
continue to temper growth

02:04.776 --> 02:07.506 align:middle
and help bring demand into
better balance with supply.

02:09.226 --> 02:11.716 align:middle
As shown in our Summary
of Economic Projections,

02:11.716 --> 02:13.976 align:middle
FOMC participants have
marked down their projections

02:13.976 --> 02:16.716 align:middle
for economic activity,
with the median projection

02:16.716 --> 02:21.336 align:middle
for real GDP growth running
below 2 percent through 2024.

02:22.726 --> 02:25.746 align:middle
The labor market has
remained extremely tight,

02:25.746 --> 02:29.716 align:middle
with the unemployment rate near
a 50 year low, job vacancies

02:29.716 --> 02:32.416 align:middle
at historical highs, and
wage growth elevated.

02:33.876 --> 02:36.706 align:middle
Over the past three months,
employment rose by an average

02:36.706 --> 02:38.676 align:middle
of 408,000 jobs per month,

02:39.266 --> 02:41.206 align:middle
down from the average
pace seen earlier

02:41.206 --> 02:43.266 align:middle
in the year but still robust.

02:45.136 --> 02:47.506 align:middle
Improvements in labor market
conditions have been widespread,

02:47.696 --> 02:49.336 align:middle
including for workers
at the lower end

02:49.336 --> 02:51.386 align:middle
of the wage distribution as well

02:51.386 --> 02:53.436 align:middle
as for African Americans
and Hispanics.

02:55.146 --> 02:56.436 align:middle
Labor demand is very strong,

02:56.786 --> 02:58.916 align:middle
while labor supply
remains subdued,

02:59.396 --> 03:01.826 align:middle
with the labor force
participation rate little

03:01.826 --> 03:03.196 align:middle
changed since January.

03:04.906 --> 03:08.416 align:middle
FOMC participants expect
supply and demand conditions

03:08.556 --> 03:10.476 align:middle
in the labor market to
come into better balance,

03:11.286 --> 03:13.856 align:middle
easing the upward pressures
on wages and prices.

03:15.696 --> 03:17.236 align:middle
The median projection in the SEP

03:17.306 --> 03:19.276 align:middle
for the unemployment
rate rises somewhat

03:19.276 --> 03:22.736 align:middle
over the next few years, moving
from 3.7 percent at the end

03:22.736 --> 03:27.506 align:middle
of this year to 4.1 percent in
2024, levels that are noticeably

03:27.506 --> 03:28.996 align:middle
above the March projections.

03:31.016 --> 03:34.296 align:middle
Inflation remains well above our
longer-run goal of 2 percent.

03:34.556 --> 03:36.116 align:middle
Over the 12 months
ending in April,

03:36.496 --> 03:39.176 align:middle
total PCE prices
rose 6.3 percent;

03:39.856 --> 03:42.176 align:middle
excluding the volatile
food and energy categories,

03:42.516 --> 03:44.626 align:middle
core prices rose 4.9 percent.

03:45.636 --> 03:48.706 align:middle
In May, the 12-month change in
the consumer price index came

03:48.706 --> 03:52.396 align:middle
in above expectations at
8.6 percent, and the change

03:52.396 --> 03:54.156 align:middle
in the core CPI was 6 percent.

03:55.686 --> 03:56.976 align:middle
Aggregate demand is strong,

03:57.356 --> 03:59.786 align:middle
supply constraints have been
larger and longer lasting

03:59.786 --> 04:02.326 align:middle
than anticipated, and
price pressures have spread

04:02.326 --> 04:04.636 align:middle
to a broad range of
goods and services.

04:06.106 --> 04:08.616 align:middle
The surge in prices of crude
oil and other commodities

04:08.616 --> 04:10.376 align:middle
that resulted from
Russia's invasion

04:10.376 --> 04:13.756 align:middle
of Ukraine is boosting
prices for gasoline and food

04:14.036 --> 04:16.876 align:middle
and is creating additional
upward pressure on inflation.

04:18.596 --> 04:21.616 align:middle
And COVID-related
lockdowns in China are likely

04:21.616 --> 04:23.946 align:middle
to exacerbate supply
chain disruptions.

04:25.396 --> 04:27.966 align:middle
FOMC participants have
revised up their projections

04:27.966 --> 04:30.086 align:middle
for inflation this
year, particularly

04:30.086 --> 04:33.026 align:middle
for total PCE inflation
given developments

04:33.026 --> 04:34.446 align:middle
in food and energy prices.

04:36.146 --> 04:39.736 align:middle
The median projection is 5.2
percent this year and falls

04:39.736 --> 04:43.686 align:middle
to 2.6 percent next year
and 2.2 percent in 2024.

04:45.136 --> 04:47.436 align:middle
Participants continue to
see risks to inflation

04:47.436 --> 04:48.866 align:middle
as weighted to the upside.

04:51.116 --> 04:53.756 align:middle
The Fed's monetary
policy actions are guided

04:53.756 --> 04:56.436 align:middle
by our mandate to
promote maximum employment

04:56.756 --> 04:58.676 align:middle
and stable prices for
the American people.

04:59.556 --> 05:01.346 align:middle
My colleagues and
I are acutely aware

05:01.346 --> 05:03.866 align:middle
that high inflation imposes
significant hardship,

05:04.196 --> 05:06.646 align:middle
especially on those least
able to meet the higher costs

05:06.646 --> 05:09.826 align:middle
of essentials like food,
housing, and transportation.

05:11.436 --> 05:14.366 align:middle
We are highly attentive to
the risks high inflation poses

05:14.366 --> 05:17.386 align:middle
to both sides of our mandate,
and we are strongly committed

05:17.386 --> 05:19.906 align:middle
to returning inflation to
our 2 percent objective.

05:22.126 --> 05:22.986 align:middle
Against the backdrop

05:22.986 --> 05:25.686 align:middle
of the rapidly evolving
economic environment,

05:25.916 --> 05:28.696 align:middle
our policy has been adapting,
and it will continue to do so.

05:29.026 --> 05:30.536 align:middle
At today's meeting,

05:30.536 --> 05:32.926 align:middle
the Committee raised the target
range for the federal funds rate

05:32.986 --> 05:35.006 align:middle
by   percentage point,

05:35.706 --> 05:38.576 align:middle
resulting in a 1½
percentage point increase

05:38.576 --> 05:40.326 align:middle
in the target range
so far this year.

05:41.896 --> 05:43.926 align:middle
The Committee reiterated
that it anticipates

05:43.926 --> 05:45.136 align:middle
that ongoing increases

05:45.136 --> 05:46.946 align:middle
in the target range
will be appropriate.

05:48.386 --> 05:49.796 align:middle
And we are continuing
the process

05:49.796 --> 05:51.556 align:middle
of significantly
reducing the size

05:51.556 --> 05:53.966 align:middle
of our balance sheet-which
plays an important role

05:53.966 --> 05:56.006 align:middle
in firming the stance
of monetary policy.

05:58.276 --> 06:00.976 align:middle
Coming out of our last meeting
in May, there was a broad sense

06:00.976 --> 06:03.506 align:middle
on the Committee that a ½
percentage point increase

06:03.506 --> 06:06.056 align:middle
in the target range should
be considered at this meeting

06:06.646 --> 06:08.796 align:middle
if economic and financial
conditions evolved

06:08.796 --> 06:10.386 align:middle
in line with expectations.

06:11.306 --> 06:13.096 align:middle
We also stated that we
were highly attentive

06:13.096 --> 06:16.386 align:middle
to inflation risks and that we
would be nimble in responding

06:16.386 --> 06:18.216 align:middle
to incoming data and
the evolving outlook.

06:19.366 --> 06:22.456 align:middle
Since then, inflation has
again surprised to the upside,

06:23.196 --> 06:25.986 align:middle
some indicators of inflation
expectations have risen,

06:26.946 --> 06:29.676 align:middle
and projections for inflation
this year have been revised

06:29.676 --> 06:30.296 align:middle
up notably.

06:32.126 --> 06:33.506 align:middle
In response to these
developments,

06:33.626 --> 06:35.576 align:middle
the Committee decided
that a larger increase

06:35.576 --> 06:38.506 align:middle
in the target range was
warranted at today's meeting.

06:39.376 --> 06:40.906 align:middle
This continues our approach

06:40.906 --> 06:43.426 align:middle
of expeditiously
moving our policy rate

06:43.426 --> 06:44.706 align:middle
up to more normal levels.

06:45.106 --> 06:46.216 align:middle
And it will help ensure

06:46.216 --> 06:48.796 align:middle
that longer-term inflation
expectations remain well

06:48.796 --> 06:49.666 align:middle
anchored at 2 percent.

06:51.486 --> 06:53.896 align:middle
As shown in the SEP,
the median projection

06:53.896 --> 06:54.876 align:middle
for the appropriate level

06:54.876 --> 06:58.576 align:middle
of the federal funds rate is 3.4
percent at the end of this year,

06:59.546 --> 07:02.886 align:middle
1.5 percentage points higher
than projected in March

07:03.456 --> 07:06.076 align:middle
and 0.9 percentage point
above the median estimate

07:06.166 --> 07:07.356 align:middle
of its longer-run value.

07:08.296 --> 07:11.146 align:middle
The median projection rises
further to 3.8 percent

07:11.146 --> 07:15.676 align:middle
at the end of next year and
declines to 3.4 percent in 2024,

07:15.966 --> 07:18.306 align:middle
still above the median
longer-run value.

07:19.486 --> 07:21.586 align:middle
Of course, these projections do
not represent a Committee plan

07:21.586 --> 07:23.176 align:middle
or decision, and no one
knows with any certainty

07:23.176 --> 07:26.616 align:middle
where the economy will be
a year or more from now.

07:27.856 --> 07:30.616 align:middle
Over coming months, we will be
looking for compelling evidence

07:30.616 --> 07:31.936 align:middle
that inflation is moving down,

07:32.456 --> 07:34.666 align:middle
consistent with inflation
returning to 2 percent.

07:36.116 --> 07:38.786 align:middle
We anticipate that ongoing rate
increases will be appropriate;

07:39.606 --> 07:41.866 align:middle
the pace of those changes
will continue to depend

07:41.866 --> 07:43.906 align:middle
on the incoming data
and the evolving outlook

07:43.906 --> 07:44.646 align:middle
for the economy.

07:45.876 --> 07:49.576 align:middle
Clearly, today's 75 basis point
increase is an unusually large

07:49.576 --> 07:52.546 align:middle
one, and I do not expect moves
of this size to be common.

07:53.966 --> 07:56.636 align:middle
From the perspective of
today, either a 50 basis point

07:56.636 --> 07:59.936 align:middle
or a 75 basis point
increase seems most likely

07:59.936 --> 08:00.726 align:middle
at our next meeting.

08:01.696 --> 08:03.906 align:middle
We will, however, make our
decisions meeting by meeting,

08:03.906 --> 08:04.826 align:middle
and we will continue

08:04.966 --> 08:07.336 align:middle
to communicate our thinking
as clearly as we can.

08:08.756 --> 08:11.396 align:middle
Our overarching focus
is using our tools

08:11.396 --> 08:14.186 align:middle
to bring inflation back
down to our 2 percent goal

08:14.226 --> 08:16.646 align:middle
and to keep longer-term
inflation expectations

08:16.646 --> 08:17.276 align:middle
well anchored.

08:19.036 --> 08:20.636 align:middle
Making appropriate
monetary policy

08:20.636 --> 08:23.206 align:middle
in this uncertain environment
requires a recognition

08:23.646 --> 08:26.136 align:middle
that the economy often
evolves in unexpected ways.

08:27.396 --> 08:30.786 align:middle
Inflation has obviously
surprised to the upside

08:30.786 --> 08:34.026 align:middle
over the past year, and further
surprises could be in store.

08:35.356 --> 08:37.356 align:middle
We therefore will need to
be nimble in responding

08:37.356 --> 08:39.306 align:middle
to incoming data and
the evolving outlook.

08:40.516 --> 08:42.576 align:middle
And we will strive to
avoid adding uncertainty

08:42.576 --> 08:45.566 align:middle
in what is already an
extraordinarily challenging

08:45.566 --> 08:46.396 align:middle
and uncertain time.

08:47.936 --> 08:51.846 align:middle
We are highly attentive to
inflation risks and determined

08:51.846 --> 08:54.406 align:middle
to take the measures necessary
to restore price stability.

08:55.546 --> 08:59.586 align:middle
The American economy is very
strong and well positioned

08:59.586 --> 09:01.046 align:middle
to handle tighter
monetary policy.

09:03.126 --> 09:06.046 align:middle
To conclude: We understand that
our actions affect communities,

09:06.046 --> 09:07.926 align:middle
families, and businesses
across the country.

09:08.616 --> 09:10.986 align:middle
Everything we do is in
service to our public mission.

09:11.956 --> 09:13.896 align:middle
We at the Fed will
do everything we can

09:14.316 --> 09:16.296 align:middle
to achieve our
maximum-employment

09:16.296 --> 09:17.566 align:middle
and price-stability goals.

09:18.236 --> 09:19.006 align:middle
Thank you.

09:19.136 --> 09:20.536 align:middle
And I look forward
to your questions.

09:21.336 --> 09:22.356 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

09:24.066 --> 09:25.446 align:middle
Howard. HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

09:25.446 --> 09:25.746 align:middle
Thank you.

09:25.746 --> 09:27.126 align:middle
Howard Schneider with Reuters.

09:27.126 --> 09:28.256 align:middle
Two related questions.

09:28.256 --> 09:31.106 align:middle
Chair Powell, do you
feel you boxed yourself

09:31.106 --> 09:33.486 align:middle
in with the language you used
at the last press conference

09:33.486 --> 09:36.356 align:middle
on 50 basis point
hikes in June and July?

09:36.876 --> 09:39.996 align:middle
And would you please give us
as detailed a sense as you can

09:39.996 --> 09:41.416 align:middle
of what role you played

09:42.156 --> 09:47.576 align:middle
in reshaping market expectations
so quickly on Monday?

09:48.016 --> 09:48.556 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

09:48.656 --> 09:54.846 align:middle
So, as you know, we always
aim to provide as much clarity

09:54.846 --> 09:57.956 align:middle
as we can about our
policy intentions subject

09:57.956 --> 10:00.616 align:middle
to the inherent uncertainty
in the economic outlook,

10:00.776 --> 10:02.786 align:middle
because we think monetary
policy is more effective

10:02.836 --> 10:05.856 align:middle
when market participants
understand how policy will

10:06.066 --> 10:08.616 align:middle
evolve, when they understand
our objective function,

10:08.856 --> 10:09.726 align:middle
our reaction function.

10:10.876 --> 10:14.056 align:middle
And in the current highly
unusual circumstances

10:14.056 --> 10:16.536 align:middle
with inflation well above our
goal, we think it's helpful

10:16.536 --> 10:20.006 align:middle
to provide even more
clarity than usual-again,

10:20.416 --> 10:23.956 align:middle
subject to uncertainty
in the outlook.

10:23.956 --> 10:27.466 align:middle
And I think over the
course of this year,

10:28.726 --> 10:32.756 align:middle
financial markets have
responded and have, generally,

10:32.756 --> 10:36.016 align:middle
shown that they understand
the path we're laying out.

10:37.166 --> 10:38.926 align:middle
Of course, it remains
data dependent.

10:39.186 --> 10:43.536 align:middle
And so that's what we
generally think about guidance,

10:43.536 --> 10:44.576 align:middle
and that's why we offer it.

10:45.276 --> 10:47.026 align:middle
And, of course, when we
offered that-when I offered

10:47.026 --> 10:48.916 align:middle
that guidance at
the last meeting,

10:49.556 --> 10:52.376 align:middle
I did say that it was subject
to the economy performing

10:52.376 --> 10:53.936 align:middle
about in line with expectations.

10:54.686 --> 11:00.586 align:middle
I also said that if the economy
performed-if data came in worse

11:00.586 --> 11:03.706 align:middle
than expected, then we
would consider moving even

11:03.706 --> 11:04.416 align:middle
more aggressively.

11:05.096 --> 11:11.316 align:middle
So we got the CPI data
and also some data

11:11.316 --> 11:15.906 align:middle
on inflation expectations
late last week, and we thought

11:16.446 --> 11:17.526 align:middle
for a while, and
we thought, well,

11:17.526 --> 11:18.816 align:middle
this is the appropriate
thing to do.

11:19.496 --> 11:21.026 align:middle
So then the question
is, what do you do?

11:21.656 --> 11:24.546 align:middle
And do you wait six weeks to
do it at the next meeting?

11:24.696 --> 11:26.496 align:middle
And I think the answer is,

11:26.556 --> 11:27.806 align:middle
that's not where
we are with this.

11:28.056 --> 11:31.066 align:middle
So we decided we needed to
go ahead, and so we did.

11:32.166 --> 11:34.916 align:middle
And that's really-that's really
how we made the decision.

11:36.276 --> 11:36.646 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

11:38.926 --> 11:39.196 align:middle
Okay. Jeanna.

11:39.366 --> 11:40.646 align:middle
JEANNA SMIALEK.

11:40.856 --> 11:42.566 align:middle
Thanks for taking our questions.

11:42.656 --> 11:44.396 align:middle
Jeanna Smialek with
the New York Times.

11:45.336 --> 11:48.696 align:middle
You-I guess I wonder
if you could describe

11:48.696 --> 11:52.156 align:middle
for us a little bit how you're
deciding how aggressive you need

11:52.156 --> 11:52.486 align:middle
to be.

11:52.566 --> 11:54.706 align:middle
So, obviously, 75 today.

11:54.706 --> 11:57.296 align:middle
What did 75 achieve
that 50 wouldn't have,

11:57.326 --> 11:59.886 align:middle
and why not just go for a full
percentage point at some point?

12:00.556 --> 12:00.896 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

12:01.206 --> 12:04.046 align:middle
Sure. So if you take a step
back, what we're looking

12:04.046 --> 12:05.506 align:middle
for is compelling evidence

12:05.506 --> 12:07.196 align:middle
that inflationary
pressures are abating

12:07.626 --> 12:09.126 align:middle
and that inflation
is moving back down.

12:09.126 --> 12:12.466 align:middle
And we'd like to see that
in the form of a series

12:12.466 --> 12:15.106 align:middle
of declining monthly inflation
readings-that's what we're

12:15.106 --> 12:15.576 align:middle
looking for.

12:16.136 --> 12:18.816 align:middle
And, by this point, we had
actually been expecting

12:18.816 --> 12:21.496 align:middle
to see clear signs of at least
inflation flattening out and,

12:21.496 --> 12:22.836 align:middle
ideally, beginning to decline.

12:23.936 --> 12:25.456 align:middle
We've said that we'd
be data dependent,

12:25.456 --> 12:27.456 align:middle
focused on incoming
data, highly attentive

12:27.456 --> 12:29.296 align:middle
to inflation risks-the
things that I mentioned

12:29.926 --> 12:32.126 align:middle
to Howard moments ago.

12:32.706 --> 12:34.446 align:middle
So contrary to expectations,

12:34.566 --> 12:36.516 align:middle
inflation again surprised
to the upside.

12:36.636 --> 12:37.946 align:middle
Indicators-some indicators

12:37.946 --> 12:41.616 align:middle
of inflation expectations
have risen, and projections

12:41.616 --> 12:43.246 align:middle
of [inflation] this year
have moved up notably.

12:43.436 --> 12:47.186 align:middle
So we thought that strong action
was warranted at this meeting,

12:47.476 --> 12:49.116 align:middle
and today we delivered
that in the form

12:49.116 --> 12:52.196 align:middle
of a 75 basis point rate
hike, as I mentioned.

12:52.196 --> 12:56.476 align:middle
So the point of it, really, is
this: We've been moving rates

12:56.826 --> 13:00.576 align:middle
up expeditiously to more normal
levels, and over the course

13:00.576 --> 13:04.546 align:middle
of the seven months since
we pivoted and began moving

13:04.546 --> 13:07.956 align:middle
in this direction, we've seen
financial conditions tighten,

13:08.066 --> 13:08.976 align:middle
and appropriately so.

13:09.766 --> 13:11.506 align:middle
But the federal funds rate,

13:11.866 --> 13:15.046 align:middle
even after this move,
is at 1.6 percent.

13:15.926 --> 13:20.586 align:middle
So, again, the Committee is
moving rates up expeditiously

13:20.586 --> 13:23.966 align:middle
to more normal levels, and
we came to the view that we'd

13:23.966 --> 13:27.656 align:middle
like to do a little more
front-end loading on that.

13:27.896 --> 13:30.436 align:middle
So I think that the SEP
gives you the levels

13:30.436 --> 13:31.906 align:middle
that people think
are appropriate

13:31.906 --> 13:33.026 align:middle
at given points in time.

13:33.346 --> 13:34.676 align:middle
This was really about the speed
in which we would get there.

13:34.676 --> 13:36.536 align:middle
So, as I mentioned, 75 basis
points today-I said the next

13:36.536 --> 13:38.606 align:middle
meeting could well be about
a decision between 50 and 75.

13:38.606 --> 13:40.036 align:middle
That would put us, at the
end of the July meeting,

13:40.036 --> 13:42.606 align:middle
in that range-in that
more normal range,

13:42.866 --> 13:44.856 align:middle
and that's a desirable place
to be, because you begin

13:44.856 --> 13:48.006 align:middle
to have more optionality
there about the speed

13:48.006 --> 13:49.716 align:middle
with which you would
proceed going forward.

13:50.976 --> 13:54.826 align:middle
Just talking about the SEP for
a second: What it really says is

13:54.826 --> 13:58.626 align:middle
that Committee participants
widely would like to see policy

13:58.676 --> 14:02.106 align:middle
at a modestly
restricted-restrictive level

14:02.106 --> 14:03.456 align:middle
at the end of this year.

14:03.546 --> 14:06.196 align:middle
And that's six months from now,
and so much data [can accrue]

14:06.196 --> 14:07.106 align:middle
and so much can happen,

14:07.106 --> 14:08.886 align:middle
so remember how highly
uncertain this is.

14:09.456 --> 14:12.716 align:middle
But so that is generally a
range of 3 to 3½ percent.

14:12.716 --> 14:15.716 align:middle
That's where people are, and
that's what they want to see,

14:15.816 --> 14:18.226 align:middle
knowing what they know now
and understanding that we need

14:18.226 --> 14:20.226 align:middle
to be-we need to show resolve

14:20.226 --> 14:21.296 align:middle
but also be flexible
[in responding]

14:21.296 --> 14:22.876 align:middle
to incoming data as we see it.

14:22.876 --> 14:25.276 align:middle
If things are better, we
don't need to do that much,

14:25.276 --> 14:28.446 align:middle
and if they're not, then
we either do that much

14:28.446 --> 14:29.366 align:middle
or possibly even more.

14:29.456 --> 14:31.456 align:middle
But in any case, it will
be very data dependent.

14:31.706 --> 14:33.946 align:middle
Then you're looking at next
year, and what you're seeing is,

14:33.946 --> 14:37.336 align:middle
people see more-a bit more
tightening and a range

14:37.336 --> 14:38.846 align:middle
of maybe 3½ to 4 percent.

14:38.896 --> 14:43.496 align:middle
And that's generally what people
see as the appropriate path

14:44.376 --> 14:47.996 align:middle
for getting inflation under
control and starting back down

14:47.996 --> 14:49.446 align:middle
and getting back
down to 2 percent.

14:50.206 --> 14:53.766 align:middle
So 75 basis points seemed
like the right thing to do

14:53.766 --> 14:56.826 align:middle
at this meeting, and
that's what we did.

14:56.826 --> 14:57.476 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

14:58.966 --> 15:01.256 align:middle
Steve. STEVE LIESMAN.

15:01.256 --> 15:01.866 align:middle
Steve Liesman, CNBC.

15:01.866 --> 15:04.126 align:middle
Thank you for taking my
question, Mr. Chairman.

15:04.206 --> 15:07.336 align:middle
You have not used the phrase in
a long time "monetary policy is

15:07.336 --> 15:09.246 align:middle
in a good place,"
which is a phrase

15:09.246 --> 15:10.986 align:middle
that you used to use often.

15:12.086 --> 15:15.426 align:middle
Now that the Committee
is projecting 4 percent

15:15.566 --> 15:19.426 align:middle
on a-or 3.8 percent next year
in terms of the funds rate,

15:19.426 --> 15:21.286 align:middle
which is similar to
where the market is now,

15:21.286 --> 15:23.626 align:middle
the futures market of 4
percent funds rate next year,

15:24.516 --> 15:27.236 align:middle
do you think that's
a level that is going

15:27.236 --> 15:30.686 align:middle
to be sufficiently high
enough to deal with and bring

15:30.686 --> 15:31.956 align:middle
down the inflation problem?

15:32.366 --> 15:35.586 align:middle
And just as a follow-up, could
you break that apart for me?

15:35.586 --> 15:37.916 align:middle
How much of that is
restrictive, and how much

15:37.916 --> 15:39.646 align:middle
of that is a normal positive
rate that ought to be embedded

15:39.646 --> 15:41.086 align:middle
or not, in your opinion,
in the funds rate?

15:41.116 --> 15:41.466 align:middle
Thank you.

15:41.816 --> 15:42.286 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

15:42.506 --> 15:44.246 align:middle
Sure. So the question really is,

15:44.716 --> 15:47.306 align:middle
how high does the rate
really need to go?

15:47.306 --> 15:49.876 align:middle
And this is-the estimates
on the Committee are

15:49.906 --> 15:52.196 align:middle
in that range of
3½ to 4 percent.

15:53.286 --> 15:54.566 align:middle
And how do you think about that?

15:54.566 --> 15:57.566 align:middle
Well, you can think about
the longer-run neutral rate,

15:57.836 --> 16:00.486 align:middle
you can compare it to that, and
we think that's in the mid-2s.

16:00.976 --> 16:04.076 align:middle
You can look, frankly, at
broader financial conditions.

16:04.076 --> 16:06.356 align:middle
You can look at asset
prices, you can look

16:06.356 --> 16:08.716 align:middle
at the effect they're having
on the economy, rates,

16:09.046 --> 16:12.576 align:middle
asset prices, credit spreads-all
of those things go into that.

16:12.986 --> 16:15.296 align:middle
You can also look at
the yield curve and ask,

16:15.296 --> 16:18.486 align:middle
all along the yield curve,
where is the policy rate?

16:18.536 --> 16:22.486 align:middle
So for much of the yield curve
now, real rates are positive.

16:22.886 --> 16:24.686 align:middle
That's not true at
the short end.

16:24.686 --> 16:27.526 align:middle
At the short end of the yield
curve in the early years,

16:27.886 --> 16:30.646 align:middle
you don't have real-you
have negative rates still.

16:31.146 --> 16:34.936 align:middle
So that really is one
data point-it's one part

16:34.936 --> 16:35.996 align:middle
of financial conditions.

16:35.996 --> 16:38.926 align:middle
So I think-I have to
look at it this way:

16:39.346 --> 16:40.716 align:middle
We move the policy rate.

16:40.876 --> 16:42.436 align:middle
That affects financial
conditions.

16:42.576 --> 16:43.956 align:middle
And that affects the economy.

16:44.536 --> 16:47.806 align:middle
We have, of course,
ways-rigorous ways to think

16:47.806 --> 16:50.306 align:middle
about it, but, ultimately,
it comes down to,

16:50.956 --> 16:52.986 align:middle
do we think financial
conditions are in a place

16:52.986 --> 16:56.086 align:middle
where they're having the
desired effect on the economy?

16:56.086 --> 17:01.286 align:middle
And that desired affect is, we'd
like to see demand moderating.

17:01.616 --> 17:03.616 align:middle
Demand is very hot
still in the economy.

17:04.026 --> 17:07.096 align:middle
We'd like to see the labor
market getting better in balance

17:07.206 --> 17:09.646 align:middle
between supply and demand, and
that can happen [as a result

17:09.646 --> 17:11.616 align:middle
of developments coming]
both from supply and demand.

17:11.616 --> 17:14.136 align:middle
Right now, there's-demand
is substantially higher

17:14.136 --> 17:17.656 align:middle
than available supply, though,
so we feel that there's a role

17:17.656 --> 17:19.076 align:middle
for us in moderating demand.

17:19.076 --> 17:21.796 align:middle
Those are the things we can
affect with our policy tools.

17:22.036 --> 17:25.946 align:middle
There are many things we can't
affect, and those would be,

17:26.216 --> 17:28.576 align:middle
you know, the things-the
commodity price issues

17:28.576 --> 17:32.396 align:middle
that we're having around the
world due to the war in Ukraine

17:32.396 --> 17:35.826 align:middle
and the fallout from
that, and also just all

17:35.826 --> 17:36.896 align:middle
of the supply-side things

17:36.896 --> 17:40.036 align:middle
that are still pushing
upward on inflation.

17:40.536 --> 17:42.596 align:middle
So that's really how I
think-how I think about it.

17:42.596 --> 17:43.096 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

17:43.096 --> 17:45.886 align:middle
But does 3.8 percent,
4 percent get it done?

17:46.036 --> 17:49.986 align:middle
Does it get the job done on
breaking the back of inflation?

17:49.986 --> 17:50.336 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

17:50.336 --> 17:54.826 align:middle
I think it's certainly in the
range of plausible numbers.

17:54.826 --> 17:56.606 align:middle
I think we'll know when
we get there really.

17:56.606 --> 17:57.566 align:middle
I mean, honestly, though,

17:57.566 --> 18:00.996 align:middle
that would be-you would have
positive real rates, I think,

18:00.996 --> 18:02.406 align:middle
and inflation coming
down by then.

18:02.406 --> 18:04.436 align:middle
I think you'd have positive real
rates across the [yield] curve.

18:05.196 --> 18:08.586 align:middle
I think that the neutral rate
is pretty low these days.

18:09.106 --> 18:11.826 align:middle
So I would think it
would, but you know what?

18:12.036 --> 18:14.216 align:middle
We're going to find
that out empirically.

18:14.266 --> 18:17.326 align:middle
We're not going to be completely
model driven about this.

18:17.326 --> 18:20.776 align:middle
We're going to be looking at
this, keeping our eyes open,

18:20.776 --> 18:23.936 align:middle
and reacting to incoming data
both on financial conditions

18:23.936 --> 18:25.916 align:middle
and on what's happening
in the economy.

18:27.476 --> 18:28.496 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

18:32.576 --> 18:33.166 align:middle
Nick. NICK TIMIRAOS.

18:33.336 --> 18:34.216 align:middle
Thanks. Nick Timiraos.

18:34.436 --> 18:36.486 align:middle
Chair Powell, you've said
that you like your policy

18:36.486 --> 18:37.886 align:middle
to work through expectations.

18:37.886 --> 18:38.746 align:middle
And now, obviously,

18:38.746 --> 18:41.106 align:middle
this decision was something
quite different from how you

18:41.106 --> 18:43.466 align:middle
and almost all of your
colleagues had set those

18:43.466 --> 18:45.426 align:middle
expectations during the
intermeeting period.

18:45.906 --> 18:46.896 align:middle
And I know you just said

18:46.896 --> 18:49.646 align:middle
that what changed was really
the inflation data-the inflation

18:49.646 --> 18:51.056 align:middle
expectations data.

18:51.056 --> 18:53.426 align:middle
But I'm wondering, on the
inflation expectations data,

18:53.846 --> 18:57.156 align:middle
was there something you saw
that was unsettling enough

18:57.546 --> 19:00.396 align:middle
to risk eroding the credibility
of your verbal guidance

19:00.396 --> 19:01.986 align:middle
by doing something so different

19:02.336 --> 19:04.496 align:middle
from what you had
"socialized" before?

19:04.716 --> 19:05.556 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

19:05.556 --> 19:08.816 align:middle
So if you look at a broad range

19:08.816 --> 19:12.736 align:middle
of inflation expectations-so
you've got the public,

19:12.736 --> 19:15.356 align:middle
you've got surveys of the
public and of experts,

19:15.356 --> 19:16.876 align:middle
and you've also got
market based.

19:16.876 --> 19:20.026 align:middle
And I think if you look across
that broad range of data,

19:20.486 --> 19:24.616 align:middle
what you see is that
expectations are still

19:24.616 --> 19:26.446 align:middle
in the place, very
much in the place,

19:26.766 --> 19:31.456 align:middle
where short-term inflation
is going to be high but comes

19:31.456 --> 19:33.796 align:middle
down sharply over the
next couple of years.

19:33.796 --> 19:35.776 align:middle
And that's really where
inflation expectations are.

19:35.776 --> 19:39.306 align:middle
And also, as you get away from
this episode, they get back

19:39.306 --> 19:40.296 align:middle
down close to 2 percent.

19:40.616 --> 19:43.466 align:middle
And so this is really
very important

19:43.466 --> 19:44.916 align:middle
to us-that that remain the case.

19:44.916 --> 19:47.346 align:middle
And I think if you
look for most measures,

19:47.466 --> 19:49.186 align:middle
most of the time,
that's what you see.

19:49.986 --> 19:53.356 align:middle
If we even see a couple
of indicators that bring

19:53.356 --> 19:56.126 align:middle
that into question, we
take that very seriously.

19:56.366 --> 19:57.236 align:middle
We do not take this

19:57.236 --> 19:58.866 align:middle
for granted-we take
it very seriously.

19:59.286 --> 20:01.466 align:middle
So the preliminary Michigan
[survey] reading-it's a

20:01.466 --> 20:03.966 align:middle
preliminary reading; it
might be revised-nonetheless,

20:04.016 --> 20:06.936 align:middle
it was quite eye-catching,
and we noticed that.

20:07.266 --> 20:10.946 align:middle
We also noticed that the index
of common inflation expectations

20:10.946 --> 20:14.586 align:middle
at the Board has moved up
after being pretty flat

20:14.586 --> 20:16.096 align:middle
for a long time, so
we're watching that,

20:16.166 --> 20:18.476 align:middle
and we're thinking, "this
is something we need

20:18.476 --> 20:19.166 align:middle
to take seriously."

20:19.286 --> 20:21.296 align:middle
And that is one of the
factors, as I mentioned-one

20:21.296 --> 20:23.406 align:middle
of the factors in our
deciding to move ahead

20:23.406 --> 20:26.476 align:middle
with 75 basis points
today was what we saw

20:26.696 --> 20:28.296 align:middle
in inflation expectations.

20:28.296 --> 20:32.496 align:middle
We're absolutely determined to
keep them anchored at 2 percent.

20:32.996 --> 20:35.526 align:middle
That was one of the reasons-the
other was just the CPI reading.

20:35.526 --> 20:36.116 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

20:36.766 --> 20:39.906 align:middle
So if you saw a movement like
that again, another tick-up

20:40.296 --> 20:43.926 align:middle
in inflation expectations,
would that put a 75

20:43.926 --> 20:47.376 align:middle
or even 100 basis point increase
in play at your next meeting?

20:48.216 --> 20:49.386 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

20:49.386 --> 20:51.146 align:middle
We're going to-I'll just
say, we're going to react

20:51.146 --> 20:54.986 align:middle
to the incoming data and
appropriately, I think.

20:55.156 --> 20:58.036 align:middle
So I wouldn't want to put a
number on what that might be.

20:58.466 --> 21:03.256 align:middle
The main thing is to get rates
up, and then pretty soon,

21:03.256 --> 21:07.506 align:middle
we'll be in an area where
we're-I think as you get closer

21:07.506 --> 21:09.726 align:middle
to the end of the
year, you're in a range

21:09.726 --> 21:13.036 align:middle
where you've got restrictive
policy, which is appropriate.

21:13.226 --> 21:16.216 align:middle
Forty-year highs in inflation-we
think that policy is going

21:16.216 --> 21:19.126 align:middle
to need to be restrictive, and
we don't know how restrictive.

21:19.366 --> 21:21.686 align:middle
So I think that's
how we'll take it.

21:22.486 --> 21:23.316 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

21:23.406 --> 21:26.136 align:middle
Neil. NEIL IRWIN.

21:26.326 --> 21:27.166 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

21:27.166 --> 21:28.146 align:middle
Neil Irwin from Axios.

21:28.146 --> 21:29.276 align:middle
Thanks for taking our questions.

21:30.356 --> 21:32.246 align:middle
The late-breaking
kind of decision to go

21:32.246 --> 21:34.306 align:middle
to 75 basis points-do you worry

21:34.306 --> 21:36.586 align:middle
that that will make policy
guidance a less effective tool

21:36.586 --> 21:37.136 align:middle
in the future?

21:37.586 --> 21:39.276 align:middle
And should we think
of that as a kind

21:39.276 --> 21:42.426 align:middle
of symmetrical reaction function
if we start to get soft readings

21:42.426 --> 21:45.256 align:middle
on inflation or if the labor
market starts to roll over?

21:46.406 --> 21:46.836 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

21:47.256 --> 21:48.716 align:middle
To take your second
question first, yes.

21:48.856 --> 21:52.476 align:middle
I mean, I think we're-again,
we're going, we're resolved

21:52.726 --> 21:54.976 align:middle
to take this on, but
we're going to be flexible

21:55.286 --> 21:56.426 align:middle
in the implementation of it.

21:56.976 --> 21:58.836 align:middle
Sorry-and your [first]
question was [about] guidance.

22:02.326 --> 22:06.416 align:middle
So, again, the overall exercise
is that we try to be-provide

22:06.416 --> 22:09.656 align:middle
as much clarity about our
policy intentions as we can,

22:09.806 --> 22:12.146 align:middle
because we think that makes
monetary policy work better.

22:12.416 --> 22:15.106 align:middle
There's always a tradeoff,
because you have to live

22:15.106 --> 22:18.656 align:middle
with that guidance,
and so you do it,

22:18.656 --> 22:19.756 align:middle
and it helps a lot of the time.

22:19.756 --> 22:22.556 align:middle
I, frankly, think this year
has been a demonstration

22:22.806 --> 22:24.676 align:middle
of how well it can work.

22:24.676 --> 22:27.916 align:middle
With us having really just
done very little in the way

22:27.916 --> 22:29.916 align:middle
of raising interest rates,

22:30.646 --> 22:33.236 align:middle
financial conditions have
tightened quite significantly

22:33.236 --> 22:34.856 align:middle
through the expectations
channel,

22:35.186 --> 22:36.676 align:middle
as we've made it clear
what our plans are.

22:36.676 --> 22:41.836 align:middle
So I think that's been a very
healthy thing to be happening.

22:42.376 --> 22:47.406 align:middle
And I would hope that our-it's
always going to be-any guidance

22:47.406 --> 22:51.096 align:middle
that we give is always going
to be subject to things working

22:51.096 --> 22:52.416 align:middle
out about as we expect.

22:52.766 --> 22:55.366 align:middle
And, in this particular
situation, you know,

22:55.366 --> 22:57.396 align:middle
we're looking for
something specific,

22:57.396 --> 22:59.826 align:middle
and that is progress
on inflation.

23:00.036 --> 23:00.966 align:middle
We want to see progress.

23:00.966 --> 23:04.086 align:middle
We want to see-inflation can't
go down until it flattens out,

23:04.406 --> 23:06.116 align:middle
and that's what we're
looking to see.

23:06.376 --> 23:09.556 align:middle
And if we don't see that,
then that's the kind of thing

23:09.556 --> 23:13.146 align:middle
that will-even if we don't see
progress for a longer period,

23:13.146 --> 23:14.396 align:middle
that could cause us to react.

23:14.706 --> 23:15.436 align:middle
But we will.

23:15.706 --> 23:18.276 align:middle
Soon enough, we will be seeing
some progress at some point.

23:18.926 --> 23:21.296 align:middle
And we'll react [as]
appropriate to that, too.

23:21.296 --> 23:22.806 align:middle
But I would like
to think, though,

23:22.806 --> 23:25.546 align:middle
that our guidance is still
credible, but it's always going

23:25.546 --> 23:27.556 align:middle
to be conditional
on what happens.

23:28.036 --> 23:31.136 align:middle
This is an unusual
situation, to get, you know,

23:31.896 --> 23:36.016 align:middle
some data late in,
during blackout,

23:36.426 --> 23:38.166 align:middle
pretty close to-very
close to our meeting.

23:38.166 --> 23:39.356 align:middle
Very unusual to [have] one

23:39.356 --> 23:42.246 align:middle
that would actually
change the outcome.

23:42.706 --> 23:46.446 align:middle
So I've only seen-in my
10-years-plus here at the Fed,

23:46.446 --> 23:48.036 align:middle
I've only seen something
like that,

23:48.036 --> 23:50.476 align:middle
even close to that,
one or two times.

23:50.536 --> 23:53.456 align:middle
So I don't think it's something
that'll come up a great deal.

23:53.456 --> 23:54.686 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

23:54.926 --> 23:58.616 align:middle
Colby. COLBY SMITH.

23:59.806 --> 24:01.426 align:middle
Thank you so much for
taking our questions.

24:01.426 --> 24:02.926 align:middle
Colby Smith with
the Financial Times.

24:03.246 --> 24:05.316 align:middle
On the "clear and
convincing" threshold

24:05.316 --> 24:08.476 align:middle
for the inflation
trajectory, what is the level

24:08.476 --> 24:11.386 align:middle
of realized inflation
that meets that criteria?

24:11.386 --> 24:13.506 align:middle
And how is the Committee
thinking

24:13.506 --> 24:16.396 align:middle
about the potential tradeoff
of much higher unemployment

24:16.596 --> 24:19.356 align:middle
than even in what's
forecasted in the SEP

24:19.356 --> 24:23.066 align:middle
if inflation is not moderating
at this acceptable pace?

24:23.156 --> 24:23.506 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

24:23.506 --> 24:24.436 align:middle
The second part I didn't get.

24:24.436 --> 24:25.466 align:middle
COLBY SMITH.

24:26.266 --> 24:30.006 align:middle
What's the potential tradeoff
with higher unemployment

24:30.006 --> 24:32.006 align:middle
than even what's
forecasted in the SEP

24:32.006 --> 24:35.266 align:middle
if inflation is not moderating
at an acceptable pace?

24:35.476 --> 24:35.756 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

24:35.756 --> 24:40.546 align:middle
Right. So, what we
want to see is a series

24:40.546 --> 24:44.306 align:middle
of declining monthly
readings for inflation,

24:45.156 --> 24:49.126 align:middle
and we'd like to see
inflation headed down.

24:49.896 --> 24:51.976 align:middle
But, you know-and right now,

24:51.976 --> 24:54.496 align:middle
our policy rate is well
below neutral, right?

24:54.496 --> 24:59.536 align:middle
So, soon enough, we'll have our
policy rate-let's assume the

24:59.536 --> 25:01.616 align:middle
world works out about
like the SEP says:

25:01.616 --> 25:03.916 align:middle
The policy rate will be up
where we think it should be.

25:04.536 --> 25:07.386 align:middle
And then the question
would be: Do you slow down?

25:07.386 --> 25:09.766 align:middle
Does it make you-you'll be
making these judgments about,

25:09.816 --> 25:13.106 align:middle
is it appropriate now to
slow down-from 50 to 25,

25:13.106 --> 25:14.826 align:middle
let's say-or speed up?

25:14.826 --> 25:18.366 align:middle
So that's the kind of thinking
we'll be doing, and, again,

25:18.366 --> 25:22.186 align:middle
we're looking-ultimately, we're
not going to declare victory

25:22.186 --> 25:27.376 align:middle
until we see a series of these,
really see convincing evidence,

25:27.376 --> 25:30.316 align:middle
compelling evidence, that
inflation is coming down.

25:30.536 --> 25:33.636 align:middle
And that's what I mean by-that's
what it would take for us

25:33.676 --> 25:36.936 align:middle
to say, "Okay, we think
this job is done."

25:36.936 --> 25:40.316 align:middle
Because we saw-and, frankly,
we saw last year-inflation came

25:40.316 --> 25:42.636 align:middle
down over the course of the
summer and then turned right

25:42.636 --> 25:43.576 align:middle
around and went back up.

25:44.216 --> 25:47.886 align:middle
So I think we're going to be
careful about declaring victory.

25:48.236 --> 25:49.966 align:middle
But our-again, the
implementation

25:49.966 --> 25:52.376 align:middle
of our policy is
going to be flexible

25:52.966 --> 25:54.376 align:middle
and sensitive to incoming data.

25:54.376 --> 25:54.796 align:middle
COLBY SMITH.

25:55.106 --> 25:59.106 align:middle
Are you more concerned now
that to bring down inflation,

25:59.106 --> 26:01.896 align:middle
it's going to require more than
just some pain at this point?

26:03.856 --> 26:04.366 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

26:04.466 --> 26:13.206 align:middle
Again, I think that-I do
think that our objective,

26:13.756 --> 26:16.256 align:middle
and this is what's reflected
in the SEP, but our objective,

26:16.256 --> 26:18.826 align:middle
really, is to bring inflation

26:18.826 --> 26:22.326 align:middle
down to 2 percent while the
labor market remains strong.

26:23.726 --> 26:30.996 align:middle
I think that what's becoming
more clear is that many factors

26:30.996 --> 26:34.546 align:middle
that we don't control are going
to play a very significant role

26:34.546 --> 26:36.906 align:middle
in deciding whether
that's possible or not.

26:37.006 --> 26:39.926 align:middle
And there I'm thinking, of
course, of commodity prices,

26:39.926 --> 26:42.866 align:middle
the war in Ukraine, supply
chain [developments],

26:42.866 --> 26:47.006 align:middle
things like that, where we
really-the monetary policy

26:47.086 --> 26:50.186 align:middle
stance doesn't affect
those things.

26:50.886 --> 26:56.256 align:middle
But having said that,
there is a path for us

26:56.256 --> 26:58.846 align:middle
to get there-it's
not getting easier;

26:59.056 --> 27:00.906 align:middle
it's getting more challenging

27:00.906 --> 27:03.246 align:middle
because of these external
forces-and that path is

27:03.746 --> 27:08.776 align:middle
to move demand down, and you
have a lot of surplus demand.

27:08.776 --> 27:12.626 align:middle
Take, for example,
in the labor market.

27:12.626 --> 27:16.476 align:middle
So you have two job
vacancies, essentially,

27:16.476 --> 27:20.966 align:middle
for every person actively
seeking a job, and that has led

27:20.966 --> 27:24.146 align:middle
to a real imbalance
in wage negotiating.

27:24.146 --> 27:27.416 align:middle
You could get to a place
where that ratio was

27:27.506 --> 27:30.876 align:middle
at a more normal level, and
you wouldn't-you would expect

27:30.876 --> 27:33.846 align:middle
to see those wage pressures
move back down to a level

27:33.846 --> 27:36.666 align:middle
where people are still
getting healthy wage increases,

27:36.666 --> 27:39.916 align:middle
real wage increases, but at
a level that's consistent

27:39.916 --> 27:40.936 align:middle
with 2 percent inflation.

27:41.346 --> 27:43.296 align:middle
So that's a possibility.

27:43.296 --> 27:44.996 align:middle
And you could say the
same thing about some

27:44.996 --> 27:47.576 align:middle
of the product markets where
there's just excess capacity

27:48.046 --> 27:52.636 align:middle
and really where the strong
demand has gone into-sorry,

27:52.866 --> 27:54.776 align:middle
where they're capacity
constrained, right?

27:54.776 --> 27:56.476 align:middle
So you have, effectively,
what we think

27:56.476 --> 27:58.776 align:middle
of as a vertical supply
curve or close to it.

27:59.316 --> 28:01.916 align:middle
So demand comes in, and it's
very strong, and it shows

28:01.916 --> 28:04.846 align:middle
up in higher prices-not higher
quantities, not more cars,

28:04.846 --> 28:05.916 align:middle
because they can't make the cars

28:05.916 --> 28:07.156 align:middle
because they don't have
the semiconductors.

28:07.586 --> 28:09.976 align:middle
So in principle, that
could work in reverse.

28:09.976 --> 28:11.386 align:middle
When demand comes down,

28:12.026 --> 28:14.876 align:middle
you could see-and it's not
guaranteed-but you could see

28:14.876 --> 28:18.536 align:middle
prices coming down more than the
typical economic relationships

28:18.536 --> 28:20.826 align:middle
that you see in the
textbooks would suggest,

28:20.826 --> 28:24.386 align:middle
because of the unusual situation
we're in on the supply side.

28:24.676 --> 28:25.796 align:middle
So there's a pathway there.

28:26.366 --> 28:33.356 align:middle
It is not going to be easy, and
there-again, it's our objective,

28:33.646 --> 28:38.026 align:middle
but, as I mentioned, it's
going to depend to some extent

28:38.026 --> 28:39.026 align:middle
on factors we don't control.

28:40.816 --> 28:42.066 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

28:42.816 --> 28:43.766 align:middle
Rachel. RACHEL SIEGEL.

28:44.006 --> 28:44.616 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

28:44.616 --> 28:45.976 align:middle
Thank you for taking
our questions.

28:45.976 --> 28:47.486 align:middle
Rachel Siegel from
the Washington Post.

28:48.016 --> 28:50.656 align:middle
So the new projections show
the unemployment rate ticking

28:50.656 --> 28:52.146 align:middle
up through 2024.

28:52.656 --> 28:55.036 align:middle
Is a higher unemployment
rate necessary

28:55.036 --> 28:56.416 align:middle
in order to combat inflation?

28:56.486 --> 28:59.266 align:middle
And what is lost if the
unemployment rate has to go up

28:59.706 --> 29:01.686 align:middle
and people lose their jobs in
order to control inflation?

29:02.096 --> 29:03.126 align:middle
Thank you.

29:03.296 --> 29:04.276 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

29:04.886 --> 29:07.186 align:middle
So you're right.

29:07.186 --> 29:09.826 align:middle
In the SEP, we have
unemployment going

29:09.826 --> 29:13.196 align:middle
up to 4 point-the median
is 4.1 percent-of course,

29:13.196 --> 29:16.406 align:middle
[there is] a range
of actual forecasts.

29:16.926 --> 29:20.466 align:middle
And I would characterize that,
if you were to get inflation

29:20.466 --> 29:24.666 align:middle
down to, you know, on
its way down to 2 percent

29:24.836 --> 29:28.416 align:middle
and the unemployment rate
went up to 4.1 percent,

29:28.516 --> 29:32.286 align:middle
that's still a historically
low level.

29:32.286 --> 29:34.356 align:middle
We hadn't seen-we
hadn't seen rates,

29:34.356 --> 29:36.956 align:middle
unemployment rates
below 4 percent

29:37.966 --> 29:40.946 align:middle
until a couple years ago
for-we'd seen it for, like,

29:40.946 --> 29:43.196 align:middle
one year in the last 50.

29:43.826 --> 29:47.726 align:middle
So the idea that-3.6
percent is historically low

29:47.726 --> 29:48.916 align:middle
in the last century.

29:48.916 --> 29:52.936 align:middle
So a 4.1 percent unemployment
rate with inflation well

29:52.936 --> 29:55.496 align:middle
on its way to 2 percent-I
think that would be-I think

29:55.496 --> 29:57.646 align:middle
that would be a successful
outcome.

29:58.156 --> 30:01.796 align:middle
So we're not looking to have
a higher unemployment rate.

30:01.876 --> 30:04.476 align:middle
But I would say that I
would certainly look at that

30:04.476 --> 30:06.046 align:middle
as a successful outcome.

30:06.276 --> 30:07.086 align:middle
RACHEL SIEGEL.

30:07.496 --> 30:09.346 align:middle
Would that include
people losing their jobs?

30:09.466 --> 30:10.316 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

30:11.276 --> 30:14.616 align:middle
We're not-again, we're not,
we don't seek to put people

30:14.616 --> 30:16.116 align:middle
out of work, of course.

30:16.116 --> 30:18.006 align:middle
We never think, "Too
many people are working,

30:18.006 --> 30:19.816 align:middle
and fewer people
need to have jobs."

30:20.476 --> 30:24.756 align:middle
But we also think that you
really cannot have the kind

30:24.756 --> 30:27.996 align:middle
of labor market we want
without price stability.

30:28.406 --> 30:31.556 align:middle
And we have to go back and
establish price stability

30:31.556 --> 30:33.746 align:middle
so we can have that
kind of labor market,

30:33.746 --> 30:36.346 align:middle
and that's a labor market

30:36.996 --> 30:40.406 align:middle
where workers are getting wage
increases-maybe the workers

30:40.406 --> 30:42.476 align:middle
at the lower end of the spectrum
are getting the biggest wage

30:42.476 --> 30:46.876 align:middle
increases, as they were before
the pandemic-where participation

30:46.876 --> 30:49.666 align:middle
is high, where there's
lots of job opportunities,

30:49.666 --> 30:51.116 align:middle
where it's just a really-I mean,

30:51.116 --> 30:53.846 align:middle
the labor market we had before
the pandemic was-that's what we

30:53.846 --> 30:54.616 align:middle
want to get back to.

30:54.956 --> 31:00.586 align:middle
And you see disparities between
various groups at historic lows.

31:00.676 --> 31:01.976 align:middle
We'd love to get
back to that place.

31:02.546 --> 31:04.626 align:middle
But, to get there-it's
not going to happen

31:04.626 --> 31:06.486 align:middle
with the levels of
inflation we have.

31:06.486 --> 31:11.666 align:middle
So we have to restore that,
and it really is in service,

31:12.256 --> 31:13.516 align:middle
in the medium and longer term,

31:13.516 --> 31:17.206 align:middle
of the kind of labor market
we want and hope to achieve.

31:17.236 --> 31:18.396 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

31:18.756 --> 31:21.906 align:middle
Matt. MATTHEW BOESLER.

31:23.056 --> 31:24.106 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

31:24.106 --> 31:26.106 align:middle
Matthew Boesler with Bloomberg.

31:26.106 --> 31:27.656 align:middle
So, as you just mentioned,

31:27.656 --> 31:29.986 align:middle
the Committee is now projecting
a ½ percentage point rise

31:29.986 --> 31:33.176 align:middle
in the unemployment rate and
the SEPs over the next couple

31:33.176 --> 31:35.936 align:middle
of years, and it removed a
line from its policy statement

31:35.936 --> 31:39.366 align:middle
about thinking that the labor
market can remain strong while

31:39.366 --> 31:40.316 align:middle
it tightens policy.

31:40.316 --> 31:43.876 align:middle
You just mentioned that that is
still your objective, though,

31:43.876 --> 31:46.476 align:middle
so I'm wondering if
you could explain why

31:46.476 --> 31:50.056 align:middle
that line was removed from the
statement-also whether this

31:50.056 --> 31:53.566 align:middle
means the FOMC is trying
to induce a recession now

31:53.906 --> 31:54.886 align:middle
to bring inflation down.

31:54.886 --> 31:55.436 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

31:55.436 --> 31:57.636 align:middle
Not trying to induce
a recession now.

31:57.866 --> 31:58.796 align:middle
Let's be clear about that.

31:58.796 --> 32:02.116 align:middle
We're trying to achieve 2
percent inflation consistent

32:02.116 --> 32:02.986 align:middle
with a strong labor market.

32:02.986 --> 32:04.156 align:middle
That's what we're trying to do.

32:04.586 --> 32:06.406 align:middle
So let me talk about
that sentence.

32:07.186 --> 32:08.736 align:middle
Clearly, it's our goal to bring

32:08.736 --> 32:11.196 align:middle
about 2 percent inflation while
keeping the labor market strong,

32:11.286 --> 32:11.486 align:middle
right?

32:11.486 --> 32:13.936 align:middle
And that's kind of
what the SEP says.

32:13.936 --> 32:17.636 align:middle
The SEP has inflation
getting down to 2,

32:17.636 --> 32:20.016 align:middle
a little above 2
percent in 2024,

32:20.016 --> 32:22.316 align:middle
with unemployment
at 4.1 percent.

32:22.316 --> 32:24.286 align:middle
And this is a strong labor
market-this is a good

32:24.286 --> 32:24.806 align:middle
labor market.

32:25.526 --> 32:27.836 align:middle
And, as I mentioned, there
are pathways to do it.

32:28.466 --> 32:31.136 align:middle
But those pathways have become
much more challenging due

32:31.136 --> 32:32.846 align:middle
to factors that are
not under our control.

32:33.016 --> 32:35.926 align:middle
Again, thinking here of the
fallout from the war in Ukraine,

32:36.456 --> 32:39.536 align:middle
which has brought a spike
in prices of energy, food,

32:39.626 --> 32:41.256 align:middle
fertilizer, industrial
chemicals,

32:42.006 --> 32:44.176 align:middle
and also just the supply
chains more broadly,

32:44.176 --> 32:46.986 align:middle
which have been larger and
longer lasting than anticipated.

32:46.986 --> 32:50.336 align:middle
So the sentence that we
deleted said that we believe

32:50.336 --> 32:53.746 align:middle
that appropriate monetary
policy, effectively alone,

32:53.896 --> 32:59.416 align:middle
can bring about the result
of 2 percent inflation

32:59.416 --> 33:00.386 align:middle
with a strong labor market.

33:01.036 --> 33:05.136 align:middle
And so much of it is really
not down to monetary policy.

33:05.136 --> 33:07.756 align:middle
It just didn't-the sentence
isn't-it kind of says

33:07.756 --> 33:10.396 align:middle
on its face that monetary
policy alone can do this.

33:11.016 --> 33:13.296 align:middle
And that's not-that just
didn't seem appropriate,

33:13.296 --> 33:14.146 align:middle
so we took the sentence out.

33:14.146 --> 33:14.936 align:middle
MATTHEW BOESLER.

33:15.056 --> 33:17.366 align:middle
And given the new projections
for the unemployment rate,

33:17.366 --> 33:19.306 align:middle
could you talk a little
bit about what accounts

33:19.306 --> 33:22.986 align:middle
for such reduced confidence
against, say, a month ago

33:22.986 --> 33:26.776 align:middle
or three months ago that
inflation will largely normalize

33:26.776 --> 33:30.086 align:middle
on its own as these supply-side
issues get worked out?

33:30.296 --> 33:30.996 align:middle
Thanks. CHAIR POWELL.

33:31.066 --> 33:33.246 align:middle
Well, yeah, I think
you've seen-again,

33:33.246 --> 33:36.616 align:middle
we've been expecting progress,
and we didn't get that.

33:36.616 --> 33:38.366 align:middle
We got sort of the opposite.

33:38.366 --> 33:43.226 align:middle
So I also think the situation,
really-since the consequences

33:43.226 --> 33:46.936 align:middle
of the Ukraine war become
more and more clear,

33:47.406 --> 33:49.736 align:middle
what you're seeing is
the situation getting

33:49.736 --> 33:50.426 align:middle
more difficult.

33:50.426 --> 33:53.686 align:middle
And you look around the world-I
mean, lots of countries are-lots

33:53.686 --> 33:56.526 align:middle
of countries are looking
at inflation of 10 percent,

33:57.126 --> 33:59.526 align:middle
and it's largely due
to commodities prices.

33:59.576 --> 34:03.386 align:middle
But all over the world,
you're seeing these effects.

34:03.426 --> 34:04.896 align:middle
And so-and we're
seeing them here:

34:05.016 --> 34:07.976 align:middle
gas prices at all-time
highs, and things like that.

34:08.416 --> 34:10.936 align:middle
That's not something we
can do something about.

34:11.416 --> 34:16.746 align:middle
So that is really-and, by
the way, headline inflation,

34:16.986 --> 34:19.286 align:middle
headline inflation is
important for expectations.

34:19.536 --> 34:21.546 align:middle
People-the public's
expectations,

34:22.646 --> 34:25.876 align:middle
why would they be distinguishing
between core inflation

34:26.046 --> 34:27.126 align:middle
and headline inflation?

34:27.166 --> 34:28.606 align:middle
Core inflation is something
we [on the Committee] think

34:28.606 --> 34:32.006 align:middle
about because it is a better
predictor of future inflation.

34:32.196 --> 34:34.286 align:middle
But headline inflation is
what people experience.

34:34.286 --> 34:35.416 align:middle
They don't know what core is.

34:35.416 --> 34:35.936 align:middle
Why would they?

34:35.936 --> 34:37.006 align:middle
They have no reason to.

34:37.346 --> 34:40.556 align:middle
So that's-expectations
are very much at risk due

34:40.556 --> 34:42.106 align:middle
to high headline inflation.

34:42.106 --> 34:45.526 align:middle
So it's become-the environment
has become more difficult,

34:45.616 --> 34:49.076 align:middle
clearly, in the last four or
five months, and hence the need

34:49.156 --> 34:51.346 align:middle
for the policy actions
that we took today.

34:51.346 --> 34:56.856 align:middle
Hence our resolution to get
rates up and, ultimately,

34:56.856 --> 35:01.646 align:middle
get them to where we think they
need to be in coming months.

35:02.156 --> 35:02.876 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

35:04.756 --> 35:05.406 align:middle
Thanks, Chair Powell.

35:05.406 --> 35:06.936 align:middle
Edward Lawrence with
Fox Business.

35:07.176 --> 35:10.426 align:middle
I wanted to ask you-you talked
about CPI going to 8.6 percent,

35:10.426 --> 35:12.926 align:middle
that retail sales surprised
the market by falling

35:12.926 --> 35:15.806 align:middle
and then revisions to the
previous months were down.

35:16.206 --> 35:19.766 align:middle
Are you hearing from contacts
about consumers slowing spending

35:19.766 --> 35:21.066 align:middle
or changing their habits?

35:21.066 --> 35:21.656 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

35:21.896 --> 35:24.786 align:middle
So we're, of course, watching
very, very carefully for that

35:24.786 --> 35:28.606 align:middle
and looking at the
retail-the big store numbers

35:28.606 --> 35:29.416 align:middle
and all that kind of thing.

35:29.726 --> 35:34.696 align:middle
And so I-but I think the fair
summary of what we see is,

35:34.696 --> 35:36.976 align:middle
you see continuing
shifts in consumption,

35:37.496 --> 35:40.236 align:middle
you see some things
getting-sales going down,

35:40.236 --> 35:42.856 align:middle
but overall spending
is very strong.

35:42.856 --> 35:45.096 align:middle
The consumer's in really
good shape financially.

35:45.566 --> 35:46.476 align:middle
They're spending.

35:46.816 --> 35:49.186 align:middle
There's no sign of
a broader slowdown

35:49.186 --> 35:50.766 align:middle
that I can see in the economy.

35:51.026 --> 35:53.766 align:middle
People are talking about it
a lot-consumer confidence is

35:53.766 --> 35:54.316 align:middle
very low.

35:54.616 --> 35:56.466 align:middle
That's probably related
to gas prices

35:58.216 --> 36:01.516 align:middle
and also just stock prices to
some extent for other people.

36:02.106 --> 36:04.666 align:middle
But that's what we're seeing.

36:04.666 --> 36:06.526 align:middle
We're not seeing
a broad slowdown.

36:06.586 --> 36:09.466 align:middle
We see job growth
slowing, but it's still

36:09.466 --> 36:10.836 align:middle
at quite robust levels.

36:11.356 --> 36:13.496 align:middle
We see the economy slowing a bit

36:13.566 --> 36:16.646 align:middle
but still growth
levels-healthy growth levels.

36:16.836 --> 36:17.176 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

36:17.176 --> 36:19.356 align:middle
So then as you are raising
rates in this economy,

36:19.356 --> 36:21.936 align:middle
how closely are you
watching consumer spending,

36:21.936 --> 36:24.116 align:middle
or is there something-another
indicator

36:24.116 --> 36:25.156 align:middle
that you're watching
more closely?

36:25.636 --> 36:26.476 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

36:26.476 --> 36:28.946 align:middle
It would be hard to watch
anything much more closely

36:28.946 --> 36:31.246 align:middle
than we watch consumer spending,
but we watch everything.

36:31.406 --> 36:33.086 align:middle
We watch business
fixed investment,

36:33.086 --> 36:35.816 align:middle
which actually has
softened a bit.

36:35.816 --> 36:40.176 align:middle
And we watch-we're responsible
for watching everything.

36:41.906 --> 36:44.996 align:middle
Consumption is 60-some
percent of the economy,

36:44.996 --> 36:47.146 align:middle
two-thirds of the
economy, so, naturally,

36:47.146 --> 36:48.216 align:middle
we spend a lot of time on that.

36:48.216 --> 36:51.586 align:middle
And, again, there's a lot going
on-there are a lot of flows back

36:51.586 --> 36:54.586 align:middle
and forth-but, ultimately,
it does appear

36:55.006 --> 36:58.616 align:middle
that the U.S. economy
is in a strong position

36:59.196 --> 37:03.216 align:middle
and well positioned to deal
with higher interest rates.

37:03.216 --> 37:03.736 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

37:04.816 --> 37:05.196 align:middle
Michael McKee.

37:05.546 --> 37:05.946 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

37:06.706 --> 37:08.846 align:middle
Thank you, Mr. Chairman.

37:08.846 --> 37:10.726 align:middle
Michael McKee from Bloomberg
Radio and Television.

37:11.126 --> 37:16.656 align:middle
Are you targeting headline
inflation now or core inflation?

37:16.656 --> 37:20.716 align:middle
In other words, how far
would you chase oil prices

37:20.716 --> 37:22.106 align:middle
if they keep going up?

37:22.106 --> 37:23.496 align:middle
If that's going to
be the component

37:23.496 --> 37:27.546 align:middle
that drives expectations,
would you risk recession

37:28.076 --> 37:31.186 align:middle
for a headline rate if the
core rate is holding steady

37:31.556 --> 37:32.526 align:middle
or starting to go down?

37:34.086 --> 37:34.446 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

37:34.596 --> 37:37.986 align:middle
So the-we're responsible
for inflation in the law.

37:38.276 --> 37:40.026 align:middle
And inflation means
headline inflation.

37:40.026 --> 37:41.696 align:middle
So that's our ultimate goal.

37:42.316 --> 37:45.056 align:middle
We, of course, like all
central banks do, look very,

37:45.056 --> 37:46.746 align:middle
very carefully at core inflation

37:46.746 --> 37:48.826 align:middle
because it is-it's a
much better predictor,

37:49.266 --> 37:51.866 align:middle
and it's much-it's a
much better predictor

37:51.866 --> 37:52.956 align:middle
of where inflation is going,

37:52.956 --> 37:55.226 align:middle
and it's also more
relevant to our tools.

37:55.296 --> 37:57.476 align:middle
As I mentioned, the
parts that don't go

37:57.476 --> 37:59.496 align:middle
into core are mostly
outside the scope

37:59.496 --> 38:01.486 align:middle
of our tools, so
we look at that.

38:01.726 --> 38:04.846 align:middle
But it's-the current situation
is particularly difficult

38:04.846 --> 38:06.806 align:middle
because of what I
mentioned about expectations.

38:06.806 --> 38:11.286 align:middle
We can't affect, really-I
mean, the energy prices are set

38:11.346 --> 38:12.606 align:middle
by global commodity prices.

38:13.256 --> 38:15.896 align:middle
And most of food-not all of it,

38:15.896 --> 38:19.286 align:middle
but most food prices are
pretty heavily influenced

38:19.286 --> 38:20.716 align:middle
by global commodity prices, too.

38:20.716 --> 38:21.476 align:middle
Also other things.

38:21.476 --> 38:22.856 align:middle
So we can't really
have much of an effect.

38:22.856 --> 38:26.646 align:middle
But we have to be mindful
of the potential effect

38:26.646 --> 38:28.056 align:middle
on inflation expectations
[coming]

38:28.146 --> 38:29.506 align:middle
from headline [inflation
readings].

38:29.506 --> 38:31.676 align:middle
So it's a very difficult
situation to be in,

38:32.276 --> 38:36.496 align:middle
and we-again, we can't do
much about the difference

38:36.496 --> 38:37.276 align:middle
between [headline and core
inflation rates due to] the

38:37.336 --> 38:39.436 align:middle
elements that make up
headline that are not in core.

38:39.436 --> 38:40.086 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

38:40.206 --> 38:43.396 align:middle
Could I just, as a follow-up,
get a clarification on the SEP?

38:43.876 --> 38:49.606 align:middle
When the members
gave their forecasts,

38:49.606 --> 38:52.136 align:middle
when were they inserted
into the record?

38:52.246 --> 38:57.396 align:middle
Were they revised after the CPI
or Michigan numbers came out?

38:57.396 --> 39:00.476 align:middle
In other words, does the
SEP, as we have it now,

39:00.516 --> 39:04.066 align:middle
reflect the same factors
that led you to go

39:04.486 --> 39:07.956 align:middle
to a 75 basis point move?

39:07.956 --> 39:08.296 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

39:08.296 --> 39:09.866 align:middle
The SEP is of one piece.

39:09.866 --> 39:13.266 align:middle
It reflects all of
the economic readings.

39:13.266 --> 39:15.646 align:middle
It also reflects the 75
basis point increase.

39:15.646 --> 39:16.326 align:middle
This is important.

39:16.326 --> 39:19.036 align:middle
So people had that in hand

39:19.536 --> 39:22.986 align:middle
when they-when their
SEPs were submitted.

39:23.586 --> 39:25.806 align:middle
So it's-in other words, it's not

39:25.806 --> 39:27.106 align:middle
in addition to what's
in the SEP.

39:27.106 --> 39:30.706 align:middle
The SEP-everyone's SEP
reflects their thinking

39:30.706 --> 39:33.426 align:middle
about this rate increase
and what's going forward.

39:34.166 --> 39:36.466 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

39:37.766 --> 39:38.736 align:middle
Victoria. VICTORIA GUIDA.

39:38.766 --> 39:40.346 align:middle
Hi, Victoria Guida
from Politico.

39:40.346 --> 39:44.276 align:middle
I wanted to ask about how
you're measuring progress,

39:44.276 --> 39:47.556 align:middle
especially since you've now
started front-loading rate

39:47.556 --> 39:48.136 align:middle
hikes more.

39:48.986 --> 39:51.616 align:middle
You've talked about how you
want to see inflation coming

39:51.616 --> 39:53.996 align:middle
down over a series of reports,

39:53.996 --> 39:57.166 align:middle
and I guess I'm curious whether
you think inflation data itself

39:57.736 --> 40:02.476 align:middle
is a really good indicator or
whether you might be concerned

40:02.476 --> 40:04.156 align:middle
that it's a lagging indicator

40:04.156 --> 40:07.906 align:middle
or that it might send
confusing signals given that,

40:07.906 --> 40:08.976 align:middle
as you've talked
about, there are sort

40:08.976 --> 40:10.756 align:middle
of supply and demand aspects.

40:11.256 --> 40:14.276 align:middle
And I guess my question
is, do you think

40:14.276 --> 40:17.416 align:middle
that inflation will tell
you-inflation data will tell you

40:17.416 --> 40:21.676 align:middle
when you've gone to where you
need to go, or do you just feel

40:21.676 --> 40:23.996 align:middle
like maybe it's better to
overshoot than to undershoot?

40:25.416 --> 40:25.806 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

40:25.806 --> 40:30.536 align:middle
So I think the role that we
can play-maybe the way to get

40:30.536 --> 40:31.596 align:middle
at this is to say that the role

40:31.596 --> 40:34.596 align:middle
that we can play is
around demand, right?

40:35.156 --> 40:37.416 align:middle
And we'll be able
to see-the areas

40:37.416 --> 40:40.736 align:middle
that we can affect are those
associated with excess demand,

40:41.396 --> 40:44.586 align:middle
and we'll be able to see
our effect on, for example,

40:44.616 --> 40:46.666 align:middle
job openings in real time.

40:46.816 --> 40:49.456 align:middle
And that would tell us
what's-that would tell us

40:49.456 --> 40:50.006 align:middle
about wages.

40:50.006 --> 40:52.216 align:middle
Wages are not principally
responsible for the inflation

40:52.216 --> 40:54.896 align:middle
that we're seeing,
but, going forward,

40:54.896 --> 40:56.606 align:middle
they would be very
important, particularly

40:56.606 --> 40:57.426 align:middle
in the service sector.

40:58.366 --> 41:03.216 align:middle
Sorry, I'm not sure I'm
getting to your question.

41:03.676 --> 41:04.606 align:middle
VICTORIA GUIDA.

41:04.606 --> 41:08.356 align:middle
My question is, is inflation
data itself the best indicator

41:08.356 --> 41:10.146 align:middle
for when you're getting
to where you need to go,

41:10.146 --> 41:12.356 align:middle
or might it lead
you to go too far?

41:12.716 --> 41:13.276 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

41:13.276 --> 41:16.156 align:middle
There's always a risk of going
too far or going not far enough,

41:16.486 --> 41:19.276 align:middle
and it's going to be a very
difficult judgment to make,

41:19.276 --> 41:21.266 align:middle
or maybe not-maybe
it'll be really clear.

41:21.876 --> 41:25.846 align:middle
But we're-and we're quite
mindful of the dangers.

41:26.016 --> 41:30.926 align:middle
But I will say, the worst
mistake we could make would be

41:30.926 --> 41:32.826 align:middle
to fail, which-it's
not an option.

41:32.826 --> 41:34.566 align:middle
We have to restore
price stability.

41:34.726 --> 41:37.846 align:middle
We really do, because
everything-it's the bedrock

41:37.846 --> 41:38.416 align:middle
of the economy.

41:38.416 --> 41:39.726 align:middle
If you don't have
price stability,

41:39.826 --> 41:40.916 align:middle
the economy's really not going

41:40.916 --> 41:42.756 align:middle
to work the way it's
supposed to.

41:42.756 --> 41:45.436 align:middle
It won't work for people-their
wages will be eaten up.

41:45.796 --> 41:47.496 align:middle
So we want to get the job done.

41:47.786 --> 41:50.176 align:middle
This inflation happened
relatively recently.

41:50.486 --> 41:53.636 align:middle
We don't think that it's
affecting expectations

41:53.636 --> 41:55.016 align:middle
in any kind of fundamental way.

41:55.016 --> 41:57.096 align:middle
We don't think that we're
seeing a wage-price spiral.

41:57.566 --> 42:04.186 align:middle
We think that the public
generally sees us as very likely

42:04.186 --> 42:05.766 align:middle
to be successful in
getting inflation

42:06.366 --> 42:08.026 align:middle
down to 2 percent,
and that's critical.

42:08.026 --> 42:09.716 align:middle
It's absolutely key
to the whole thing

42:10.276 --> 42:12.716 align:middle
that we sustain that confidence.

42:12.916 --> 42:14.586 align:middle
So that's how we're
thinking about it.

42:15.136 --> 42:16.346 align:middle
BRIAN CHEUNG.

42:20.886 --> 42:22.576 align:middle
Hi, Brian Cheung
with Yahoo Finance.

42:22.576 --> 42:26.336 align:middle
I just want to expand, I guess,
on what you just said now

42:26.336 --> 42:29.266 align:middle
about the general public feeling
like you can get this done.

42:29.726 --> 42:31.676 align:middle
You talk about consumer
sentiment being down,

42:31.896 --> 42:33.716 align:middle
also inflation expectations
being up,

42:33.986 --> 42:36.046 align:middle
recession just broadly
being dinner table talk.

42:36.666 --> 42:40.046 align:middle
Does the general feel
among American households

42:40.046 --> 42:44.116 align:middle
and also businesses square with
your explanation of the economy,

42:44.276 --> 42:46.006 align:middle
given that the description
of inflation

42:46.006 --> 42:47.816 align:middle
in the statement didn't
change between May and June?

42:48.156 --> 42:50.006 align:middle
Thanks. CHAIR POWELL.

42:50.906 --> 42:54.956 align:middle
So, clearly, people don't
like inflation-a lot.

42:55.076 --> 42:57.726 align:middle
And many people are experiencing
it, really, for the first time,

42:58.376 --> 43:01.176 align:middle
because we haven't had
anything like this kind

43:01.176 --> 43:02.646 align:middle
of inflation in 40 years.

43:02.646 --> 43:05.746 align:middle
And it's really something
people don't like.

43:05.746 --> 43:07.736 align:middle
And they're experiencing
that, and that's showing

43:07.736 --> 43:11.546 align:middle
up in their-in surveys
and in all kinds of ways.

43:12.166 --> 43:16.386 align:middle
And we understand that, and
we understand the hardship

43:16.386 --> 43:19.566 align:middle
that people are experiencing
from high inflation,

43:19.566 --> 43:21.776 align:middle
and we're determined
to do what we can

43:22.416 --> 43:23.536 align:middle
to get inflation back down.

43:24.126 --> 43:25.716 align:middle
So that's really
what we're saying.

43:25.716 --> 43:30.456 align:middle
We're not-I'm not-clearly, it's
an incredibly unpopular thing,

43:31.246 --> 43:32.986 align:middle
and it's very painful
for people.

43:32.986 --> 43:37.106 align:middle
I guess what I'm
saying is, the question,

43:37.106 --> 43:39.526 align:middle
the really critical
question from the perspective

43:39.526 --> 43:43.116 align:middle
of doing our job is making sure

43:43.376 --> 43:45.176 align:middle
that the public does
have confidence

43:45.406 --> 43:48.376 align:middle
that we have the tools and
will use them and they do work

43:49.116 --> 43:51.846 align:middle
to bring inflation
back down over time.

43:51.876 --> 43:53.506 align:middle
It will take some
time, we think,

43:53.736 --> 43:56.016 align:middle
to get inflation back
down, but we will do that.

43:57.586 --> 43:58.516 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

43:59.736 --> 44:01.006 align:middle
Chris. CHRISTOPHER RUGABER.

44:01.006 --> 44:01.386 align:middle
Thank you.

44:01.556 --> 44:03.096 align:middle
Chris Rugaber, Associated Press.

44:03.666 --> 44:05.946 align:middle
You have talked about
inflation a few times

44:05.946 --> 44:09.396 align:middle
and mentioned oil
prices, China lockdowns.

44:10.176 --> 44:14.426 align:middle
But aside from rises
in commodity prices

44:14.606 --> 44:18.256 align:middle
such as gas prices, we're
also seeing stickier measures

44:18.256 --> 44:21.616 align:middle
of inflation increasing, such
as the Cleveland Fed's median

44:21.616 --> 44:22.866 align:middle
and trimmed mean CPIs.

44:22.866 --> 44:26.716 align:middle
I mean, how persistent do you
see those underlying measures

44:26.716 --> 44:31.036 align:middle
of inflation, and how do you
expect to-where do you see those

44:31.036 --> 44:31.976 align:middle
going in the near future?

44:31.976 --> 44:32.366 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

44:32.666 --> 44:35.596 align:middle
So, as I mentioned, I think,
in my opening statement,

44:35.716 --> 44:38.636 align:middle
inflation has started-it
started off in quite narrow,

44:38.636 --> 44:42.276 align:middle
very directly pandemic-related
areas,

44:42.276 --> 44:45.516 align:middle
and it's spread now
broadly across the economy

44:45.516 --> 44:46.996 align:middle
and into the services
sector as well.

44:46.996 --> 44:48.776 align:middle
It was really in the goods
sector at the beginning.

44:49.476 --> 44:53.006 align:middle
And, particularly, you're seeing
in travel now, if you've flown

44:53.006 --> 44:55.716 align:middle
on a plane lately-planes
are very full,

44:55.716 --> 44:57.396 align:middle
and plane tickets
are very expensive.

44:58.116 --> 45:00.256 align:middle
Some of that will be
pass-through of energy prices,

45:00.326 --> 45:03.806 align:middle
but it's-so you're
experiencing services inflation.

45:04.936 --> 45:06.576 align:middle
Shelter inflation is high.

45:06.576 --> 45:12.796 align:middle
So the question-and then you see
the Cleveland measure going up,

45:12.796 --> 45:14.376 align:middle
and many other measures
are going up.

45:14.376 --> 45:17.316 align:middle
So it's a time when we're not
seeing progress and we want

45:17.316 --> 45:20.526 align:middle
to see progress, and
that's really another part

45:20.526 --> 45:22.026 align:middle
of why we did what we did today

45:22.026 --> 45:25.936 align:middle
and why the SEP looks
like it does.

45:25.936 --> 45:30.086 align:middle
We see it as appropriate
to get the policy rate

45:30.086 --> 45:33.066 align:middle
up to restrictive levels,
which would be, in the thinking

45:33.066 --> 45:34.766 align:middle
of the Committee,
somewhere in the range of 3

45:34.766 --> 45:37.036 align:middle
to 3½ percent by year-end.

45:37.036 --> 45:40.326 align:middle
And then after that, you see
what the rest of the SEP says.

45:40.526 --> 45:42.506 align:middle
So I hope that's
responsive to your question.

45:43.406 --> 45:43.766 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

45:43.826 --> 45:47.586 align:middle
Nancy. NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

45:47.676 --> 45:48.376 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

45:48.376 --> 45:50.286 align:middle
Nancy Marshall-Genzer
with Marketplace.

45:50.856 --> 45:54.716 align:middle
Do you still think a
softish landing is possible,

45:54.716 --> 45:58.076 align:middle
and how would you define that
at this point considering the

45:58.076 --> 46:03.076 align:middle
revised projections for
unemployment, GDP, inflation?

46:03.076 --> 46:04.276 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

46:04.376 --> 46:08.426 align:middle
So I think what's in the SEP
would certainly meet that test.

46:08.706 --> 46:11.856 align:middle
If you see-you're
looking at getting back

46:11.856 --> 46:15.886 align:middle
down to almost a 2
percent inflation by 2024,

46:15.886 --> 46:18.396 align:middle
and the unemployment rate is
still as low as 4.1 percent.

46:18.396 --> 46:22.856 align:middle
That would be-I would call
that as meeting that test.

46:23.406 --> 46:25.506 align:middle
Do I still think
that we can do that?

46:25.506 --> 46:29.066 align:middle
I do. I think there's-I
think there's-I don't want

46:29.066 --> 46:30.156 align:middle
to be the handicapper here.

46:30.156 --> 46:34.896 align:middle
I just-that is our objective,
and I do think it's possible.

46:35.046 --> 46:36.676 align:middle
Like I said, though,
I think that events

46:36.676 --> 46:40.196 align:middle
of the last few months have
raised the degree of difficulty,

46:40.196 --> 46:41.606 align:middle
created great challenges.

46:42.196 --> 46:44.426 align:middle
And, again, the answer
to the question,

46:44.526 --> 46:48.376 align:middle
can we still do it-there's
a much bigger chance now

46:48.376 --> 46:51.936 align:middle
that it'll depend on factors
that we don't control, which is,

46:52.296 --> 46:56.866 align:middle
fluctuations and spikes in
commodity prices could wind

46:56.866 --> 46:58.856 align:middle
up taking that option
out of our hands.

46:59.356 --> 47:04.006 align:middle
So we just don't know, but we're
focused on-very, very focused

47:04.006 --> 47:06.786 align:middle
on getting inflation
back down to 2 percent,

47:07.546 --> 47:11.286 align:middle
which we think is essential
for the benefit of the public

47:11.286 --> 47:13.796 align:middle
and also to put us
on a path back

47:13.796 --> 47:16.466 align:middle
to a sustainably
strong labor market

47:16.506 --> 47:18.006 align:middle
like the one we had
before the pandemic.

47:19.626 --> 47:21.286 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

47:21.846 --> 47:25.976 align:middle
Greg. GREG ROBB.

47:26.646 --> 47:26.946 align:middle
Thank you.

47:27.096 --> 47:28.476 align:middle
Greg Robb from MarketWatch.

47:29.136 --> 47:29.936 align:middle
Chair Powell, I was wondering

47:29.936 --> 47:33.076 align:middle
if you could talk a little bit
more-economists are worried

47:33.076 --> 47:37.016 align:middle
that you're kind of hitting
the economy with a sledgehammer

47:37.016 --> 47:40.706 align:middle
and that now there's even
more risk of a recession

47:41.516 --> 47:44.176 align:middle
than a 50-50 path of rates.

47:44.466 --> 47:45.796 align:middle
So could you talk a
little bit more about that?

47:45.796 --> 47:50.096 align:middle
And what evidence would
get you to stop rate hikes

47:50.096 --> 47:52.646 align:middle
and maybe even reverse them?

47:52.646 --> 47:53.196 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

47:53.766 --> 47:55.286 align:middle
Sure. So, as I mentioned,

47:55.286 --> 47:56.646 align:middle
financial conditions
have tightened

47:56.986 --> 48:01.216 align:middle
over the last seven months, and
that's a good thing, we think.

48:01.696 --> 48:02.816 align:middle
But the federal funds rate,

48:02.886 --> 48:06.326 align:middle
even after this increase,
is at 1.6 percent.

48:07.126 --> 48:10.136 align:middle
So it's hard to see how
that is too high of a rate.

48:10.136 --> 48:13.796 align:middle
And even if we did another-so
we're going to get here

48:13.796 --> 48:16.446 align:middle
by the end of the summer,
somewhere in the 2s probably.

48:16.836 --> 48:18.696 align:middle
Still, that's still a low rate.

48:18.856 --> 48:21.086 align:middle
So that's not a rate
that is calculated

48:21.086 --> 48:22.966 align:middle
to bring a recession on.

48:23.156 --> 48:25.806 align:middle
And we'll-by then, we'll have
seen a whole lot more data.

48:26.006 --> 48:29.876 align:middle
As I mentioned a couple times,
the Committee's views are

48:29.876 --> 48:34.656 align:middle
around a modestly restrictive
stance, which will be in the 3

48:34.656 --> 48:37.396 align:middle
to 3½ percent range by
the end of this year,

48:37.476 --> 48:41.146 align:middle
but that's conditioned on that
being the appropriate thing

48:41.146 --> 48:41.586 align:middle
to do.

48:41.796 --> 48:44.126 align:middle
If we see data going in
a different direction,

48:44.716 --> 48:47.936 align:middle
it'll be reflected in our
policy, as you see today.

48:48.366 --> 48:53.656 align:middle
We'll be watching-if things go
in a direction we don't expect,

48:53.656 --> 48:54.496 align:middle
then we're going to adapt.

48:54.496 --> 48:56.856 align:middle
And I would say, this
is a highly uncertain

48:57.246 --> 49:00.206 align:middle
environment-extraordinarily
uncertain environment.

49:00.776 --> 49:05.926 align:middle
So, again, we'll be determined
and resolved, but flexible.

49:07.746 --> 49:08.276 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

49:08.356 --> 49:09.156 align:middle
Okay. Evan.

49:11.466 --> 49:12.476 align:middle
EVAN RYSER.

49:12.516 --> 49:13.936 align:middle
Evan Ryser, Market
News International.

49:14.166 --> 49:14.866 align:middle
Thank you, Chair Powell.

49:15.306 --> 49:18.046 align:middle
I was wondering if the
Fed has initiated a review

49:18.046 --> 49:20.836 align:middle
of the conduct of monetary
policy over the last two years

49:20.836 --> 49:22.946 align:middle
or so, given the inflation,

49:23.476 --> 49:25.226 align:middle
and will that be
shared with the public?

49:25.816 --> 49:28.366 align:middle
And then, secondly,
given the illiquidity

49:28.366 --> 49:30.846 align:middle
and the extraordinary
volatility in financial markets,

49:31.106 --> 49:33.396 align:middle
are you concerned that
QT will make that worse?

49:33.566 --> 49:33.956 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

49:34.716 --> 49:36.776 align:middle
Sorry, what was your
question on QT?

49:37.146 --> 49:37.496 align:middle
EVAN RYSER.

49:37.496 --> 49:41.036 align:middle
Just given the illiquidity
and extraordinary volatility

49:41.036 --> 49:43.696 align:middle
in financial markets, whether
QT will make things worse.

49:44.326 --> 49:45.686 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

49:46.196 --> 49:51.556 align:middle
So, of course, we've been
looking very carefully and hard

49:51.706 --> 49:54.406 align:middle
at why inflation
picked up so much more

49:54.406 --> 49:57.456 align:middle
than expected last year and
why it proved so persistent.

49:58.166 --> 50:04.336 align:middle
We-it's hard to overstate the
extent of interest we have

50:04.336 --> 50:07.096 align:middle
in that question
morning, noon, and night.

50:07.946 --> 50:11.756 align:middle
But you have to understand
the context.

50:11.866 --> 50:17.956 align:middle
For-really, the context is this:
For decades before the pandemic

50:17.956 --> 50:19.996 align:middle
and the reopening,
you had a world

50:20.156 --> 50:24.326 align:middle
where inflation was dominated
by disinflationary forces

50:24.326 --> 50:28.436 align:middle
such as declining population
or aging demographics,

50:28.436 --> 50:31.616 align:middle
let's call it that,
globalization enabled

50:31.616 --> 50:34.226 align:middle
by technology, other
factors, low productivity.

50:34.936 --> 50:41.506 align:middle
So and that's how all the models
work is, decades and decades

50:41.506 --> 50:43.486 align:middle
of data-they look at that.

50:43.486 --> 50:45.086 align:middle
It's a very flat
Philips curve world,

50:45.086 --> 50:47.606 align:middle
and the supply shocks tend
to be transient, right?

50:48.346 --> 50:53.296 align:middle
So we have now experienced an
extraordinary series of shocks,

50:53.296 --> 50:56.246 align:middle
if you think about it: the
pandemic, the response,

50:56.456 --> 51:00.986 align:middle
the reopening, inflation,
followed by the war in Ukraine,

51:02.066 --> 51:05.636 align:middle
followed by shutdowns
in China-the war

51:05.636 --> 51:08.916 align:middle
in Ukraine potentially having
effects for years here.

51:09.376 --> 51:11.496 align:middle
So we're aware that
a different set

51:11.496 --> 51:13.766 align:middle
of forces are driving
the economy-we have been,

51:13.766 --> 51:15.086 align:middle
obviously, for quite a while.

51:15.086 --> 51:17.466 align:middle
But this is a different-these
forces are different.

51:17.996 --> 51:19.836 align:middle
Inflation is behaving
differently, and,

51:19.836 --> 51:24.336 align:middle
in our thinking, it really is a
question of very strong demand.

51:24.336 --> 51:28.576 align:middle
But you couldn't get this kind
of inflation without a change

51:28.576 --> 51:31.056 align:middle
on the supply side, which
is there for anybody to see,

51:31.056 --> 51:34.676 align:middle
which is these blockages and
shortages and people dropping

51:34.676 --> 51:36.546 align:middle
out of the labor force
and things like that.

51:36.546 --> 51:39.936 align:middle
So that's how we're
looking at it.

51:39.936 --> 51:42.886 align:middle
And we've done a lot
of work internally on,

51:42.936 --> 51:47.816 align:middle
and thinking about,
what all that means.

51:47.816 --> 51:48.646 align:middle
You don't-the thing is,

51:48.646 --> 51:52.606 align:middle
you don't know whether those
forces are-to what extent are

51:52.606 --> 51:53.716 align:middle
they going to be sustained?

51:54.486 --> 51:56.536 align:middle
In other words, will
we go back to a world

51:56.826 --> 51:59.116 align:middle
where it looks a little
more like the old world,

51:59.166 --> 52:00.846 align:middle
or are we really
going to be in a world

52:00.846 --> 52:03.836 align:middle
where major supply
shocks go on and on?

52:04.266 --> 52:07.256 align:middle
The history is, you see
these waves of supply shocks,

52:07.256 --> 52:10.216 align:middle
as you did in the '70s,
and then they go away,

52:10.286 --> 52:13.326 align:middle
and sort of there's a "new
normal," and things settle down.

52:13.526 --> 52:17.866 align:middle
But, honestly, we don't know
what that's going to be.

52:17.866 --> 52:20.186 align:middle
In the meantime, we have
to find price stability

52:20.186 --> 52:22.506 align:middle
in this new world and
maximum employment

52:22.506 --> 52:24.046 align:middle
in this new world
where, clearly,

52:24.696 --> 52:27.356 align:middle
inflationary forces are-you're
seeing them everywhere.

52:27.356 --> 52:29.216 align:middle
Again, if you look
around the world

52:29.216 --> 52:32.166 align:middle
at where inflation levels are,
it's absolutely extraordinary.

52:32.796 --> 52:33.796 align:middle
It's not just here.

52:33.886 --> 52:36.016 align:middle
In fact, we're sort of in
the middle of the pack,

52:36.016 --> 52:39.006 align:middle
although I think we have,
of course, a different kind

52:39.006 --> 52:42.086 align:middle
of inflation than
other people have,

52:42.166 --> 52:43.936 align:middle
partly because our
economy is stronger

52:43.936 --> 52:45.216 align:middle
and more highly recovered.

52:45.216 --> 52:46.276 align:middle
So that's what we're doing.

52:46.276 --> 52:48.576 align:middle
We've done a lot of
introspection and work

52:48.576 --> 52:55.216 align:middle
on that and-sorry, on QT, we've
communicated really clearly

52:55.216 --> 52:57.986 align:middle
to the markets about what
we're going to do there.

52:57.986 --> 52:59.626 align:middle
Markets seem to be okay with it.

52:59.686 --> 53:03.726 align:middle
We're phasing in-Treasury
[security] issuance is

53:03.726 --> 53:06.436 align:middle
down quite a lot, quite a
lot from where it's been,

53:06.986 --> 53:08.646 align:middle
so I have no reason

53:08.646 --> 53:10.776 align:middle
to think-markets
are forward looking,

53:10.776 --> 53:11.676 align:middle
and they see this coming.

53:11.676 --> 53:14.076 align:middle
I have no reason to
think it will lead

53:14.076 --> 53:15.716 align:middle
to illiquidity and problems.

53:15.946 --> 53:18.086 align:middle
It seems to be kind
of understood

53:18.086 --> 53:19.686 align:middle
and accepted at this point.

53:19.686 --> 53:20.526 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

53:20.706 --> 53:23.116 align:middle
Thanks. For the last question,
we'll go to Mark Hamrick.

53:23.406 --> 53:24.026 align:middle
MARK HAMRICK.

53:25.296 --> 53:27.506 align:middle
Mr. Chairman, Mark
Hamrick with Bankrate.

53:27.506 --> 53:30.596 align:middle
I wonder what your
assessment is about the outlook

53:30.596 --> 53:34.116 align:middle
for the housing market,
given the years-long increase

53:34.116 --> 53:38.166 align:middle
in home prices and now the
sharp rise in mortgage rates.

53:38.726 --> 53:42.216 align:middle
And all that, of course, given
the heightened sensitivity

53:42.216 --> 53:45.516 align:middle
around the housing market given
the fact that it was a trigger

53:45.516 --> 53:47.906 align:middle
for the great financial
crisis over a decade ago.

53:47.996 --> 53:48.296 align:middle
Thank you.

53:48.296 --> 53:48.716 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

53:48.716 --> 53:54.596 align:middle
Sure. So rates were very
low-a good place to start is

53:54.596 --> 53:56.896 align:middle
that rates were very,
very low for quite a while

53:57.656 --> 54:02.036 align:middle
because of the pandemic and the
need to do everything we could

54:02.096 --> 54:05.736 align:middle
to support the economy when
unemployment was 14 percent

54:05.736 --> 54:09.266 align:middle
and the true unemployment rate
was well higher than that.

54:09.946 --> 54:14.106 align:middle
And that was a-rates are low,
and now they're coming back

54:14.106 --> 54:16.216 align:middle
up to more normal,
or above, levels.

54:16.216 --> 54:19.606 align:middle
So, in the meantime,
while rates were low

54:19.606 --> 54:21.806 align:middle
and while demand was really
high, obviously demand

54:21.806 --> 54:25.246 align:middle
for housing changed from
wanting to live in urban areas

54:25.246 --> 54:28.746 align:middle
to some extent to living
in single-family homes

54:28.746 --> 54:30.746 align:middle
in the suburbs, famously.

54:30.746 --> 54:32.986 align:middle
And so the demand was
just suddenly much higher.

54:33.606 --> 54:38.716 align:middle
And we saw prices moving
up very, very strongly

54:38.716 --> 54:39.956 align:middle
for the last couple of years.

54:40.426 --> 54:44.106 align:middle
So that changes now, and rates
have moved up-we're well aware

54:44.106 --> 54:45.176 align:middle
that mortgage rates have moved

54:45.176 --> 54:48.696 align:middle
up a lot-and you're seeing
a changing housing market.

54:49.226 --> 54:52.086 align:middle
We're watching it to
see what will happen.

54:52.086 --> 54:54.426 align:middle
How much will it really
affect residential investment?

54:54.546 --> 54:55.496 align:middle
Not really sure.

54:55.846 --> 54:59.156 align:middle
How much will it
affect housing prices?

54:59.816 --> 55:01.006 align:middle
Not really sure.

55:01.006 --> 55:03.876 align:middle
It's-I mean, obviously, we're
watching that quite carefully.

55:04.176 --> 55:06.746 align:middle
You would think over
time-I mean,

55:06.746 --> 55:09.736 align:middle
so there's a tremendous amount
of supply in the housing market

55:09.736 --> 55:12.806 align:middle
of unfinished homes,
and as those come

55:12.806 --> 55:16.386 align:middle
on line-whereas the
supply of finished homes,

55:16.786 --> 55:18.466 align:middle
inventory of finished
homes that are for sale,

55:18.466 --> 55:21.296 align:middle
is incredibly low,
historically low.

55:21.376 --> 55:23.406 align:middle
So it's still a very
tight market.

55:23.986 --> 55:26.296 align:middle
So prices may keep going
up for a while even

55:26.296 --> 55:28.096 align:middle
in a world where rates are up.

55:28.676 --> 55:30.056 align:middle
So it's a complicated situation.

55:30.056 --> 55:31.656 align:middle
We watch it very carefully.

55:32.296 --> 55:36.406 align:middle
I would say if you're
a homebuyer, somebody

55:36.406 --> 55:38.176 align:middle
or a young person
looking to buy a home,

55:38.666 --> 55:40.596 align:middle
you need a bit of a reset.

55:40.596 --> 55:43.216 align:middle
We need to get back to
a place where supply

55:43.216 --> 55:46.646 align:middle
and demand are back together
and where inflation is

55:46.646 --> 55:49.986 align:middle
down low again and mortgage
rates are low again.

55:49.986 --> 55:53.476 align:middle
So this will be a process
whereby we-ideally,

55:53.666 --> 55:57.766 align:middle
we do our work in a way that
the housing market settles

55:57.766 --> 56:00.766 align:middle
in a new place and
housing availability

56:00.766 --> 56:03.476 align:middle
and credit availability
are at appropriate levels.

56:04.796 --> 56:10.946 align:middle
So thank you very much.

